lic Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") used to calculate the mean of the sample. b.) used whenever the researcher wants to adjust for confounding by the direct or indirect standardization method. c.) the sample size divided by the square root of the sample standard deviation. d.) a type of sampling bias that can lead to an erroneous study conclusion. e.) the standard deviation of the distribution of sample means. 23. Researchers want to make an inference about the average serum (blood) cholesterol levels of students in college B. They will do this by randomly sampling 500 students from college B. The researchers want to be absolutely certain that they sample 250 male students and 250 female students. The researchers should: (Select the best statement.) a.) calculate a 50% confidence interval on the sample mean. b.) use the methods of stratified sampling. c.) conduct a randomized controlled trial to help assure comparability of men and women. d.) randomly select 500 subjects, and if there are 252 men and 248 women, discard the men with the highest two serum cholesterol levels and hand-select two female students. e.) perform a stratified t-test on the average serum cholesterol levels of the males vs. the females. 24. Researchers prospectively follow a group of 100 vegetarians and 200 non-vegetarians. After 30 years, 8 of the vegetarians develop heart disease and 20 of the non-vegetarians develop heart disease. The 95% confidence interval on the relative risk of 0.8 extends from 0.6 to 0.9. Alpha was set at 0.05. Select the best statement: a.) Vegetarians were 80% less likely to develop heart disease over 30 years vs. the non-vegetarians. b.) The relative risk of 0.8 is not statistically significant as the 95% confidence interval contains the value 0.8. c.) Vegetarians were 20% less likely to develop heart disease over 30 years vs. non-vegetarians. d.) The study had 95% power to detect a true difference of 20% or more. e.) The researchers should have calculated an odds ratio rather than a relative risk. 25. The grades on exam C are normally distributed with a population mean of 75% and a population standard deviation of 5%. Select the best statement: a.) The 95% confidence interval on the exam C distribution extends from 65% to 85%. b.) 34% of the grades on exam C are between 75% and 80%. c.) 65% of the grades on exam C are between 65% and 85%. d.) The area under the entire curve of exam C grades could be less than 100%. e.) 95% of the grades on exam C are between 75% and 85%. 26. Researchers want to determine if the proportion of men from town A who smoke cigarettes is different than the proportion of women from town A who smoke cigarettes. The researchers randomly select 1,000 men and 1,200 women from town A and they ask them if they smoke cigarettes. Subjects are only permitted to answer yes or no to the question. Select the best statement: a.) Smoking could be a confounder in this study. b.) Gender could be a confounder in this study. c.) The study likely has poor internal validity as there are more women in the study than men. d.) The researchers should test their hypothesis with a two-sample t-test. e.) The researchers should test their hypothesis with a chi-square test. 27. Researchers conduct a randomized controlled trial comparing subjects on Med A vs. Med B in the prevention of strokes. They determine that 200 subjects, 100 subjects in each arm, are required to have a power of 90% to detect a true difference of 5% or more. One percent of the subjects on Med A developed a stroke while 4% of the subjects on Med B developed a stroke. Alpha was set at 0.05 and the resulting P-value was 0.07. Select the best statement:"},{"label":"A","text":") The researchers should reject the null hypothesis. b.) Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 5% or more due to chance alone is 7%. c.) Given the alternative hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 5% or more due to chance alone is 7%. d.) Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 3% or more due to chance alone is 90%. e.) Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 3% or more due to chance alone is 7%. 28 Investigators examine data from police records to identify crash factors associated with a driver fatality vs. a driver non-fatality. The crash factors they consider are driver blood alcohol level, driver age and driver use of the seat belt. This is an example of a: a.) case control study b.) retrospective cohort study c.) case series study d.) prospective cohort study e.) large case report study 29. German Measles is a potentially dangerous disease in adults. It spreads quickly unless health officials intervene immediately. The Department of Public Health (DPH) requires physicians by law to report all cases of adult German measles to them by phone. A 25-year-old graduate student reports to Dr. Mtweve in a college infirmary with symptoms and a rash that suggest German measles, but the definitive blood test won’t be available for one month. Select the best .) Dr. Mtweve should not report the case to the DPH at this point in time as it might not be German measles b.) Dr. Mtweve should wait to see if other students develop similar symptoms before reporting the case to the DPH c.) Dr. Mtweve should only report the case to the DPH if the patient has given him permission to do so d.) Dr. Mtweve should report the case to the DPH immediately and tell the patient the case is being reported e.) Dr. Mtweve should consult the World Health Organization’s case definition of German measles to assure his patient meets the case definition before reporting to the DPH"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ff5e55493f4d","stem":"18 years to determine how many developed cardiovascular disease (CVD) during that time period. One hundred and sixty men were diagnosed with cardiovascular disease; of these 160, ninety were among the 500 that were initially diagnosed with high blood pressure as well. [10 marks] a) What kind of study is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"High BP"},{"label":"B","text":"No high BP"},{"label":"C","text":"No CVD"},{"label":"D","text":"c) Using the above information, (i) Name the appropriate measure of association (2) ............................................................................................................. (ii) Calculate the appropriate measure of association and (2 marks) (iii) Interpret the results what it means: (2 marks) END OF EXAM From the Department: Best of luck with your medical career"},{"label":"E","text":"ìØÇ¶¢¶‘€¶‘¶‘o‘^¶^M¶M¶^¶M¶"},{"label":"F","text":"°Ð/ °à=!°"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ccb6eb06a08d43c947154d10367c89fbad789e84"},{"id":"q-2cac979dd1f4","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"C","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"D","text":"€place"},{"label":"E","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"F","text":"€State"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-4f8b4ab48d7f","stem":"08 per person-year What is the fraction of cases with the disease among the exposed that is attributable to the exposure? Unexposed Exposed Disease 9 17 No disease 7 5","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6dc961b7c7ae","stem":"55 An epidemiologist performed a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial to examine the effect of fish oil supplementation on memory complaints. In the group that received fish oil, 19% of the participants forgot to take their daily supplements during the study whereas this was only 3% in the placebo group. Is this a problem? Yes, because this affects the external validity of the study. Yes, because the study outcome can be biased. No, because participants and investigators were blinded towards the type of treatment in both groups No, because all confounders were equally distributed over both groups due to randomization An epidemiologist wants to assess the effect of tea drinking on blood pressure. He decides to do an intervention study. Which of the following measures does not increase the internal validity of the study? Careful monitoring of blood pressure during the study Inclusion of a control group Randomization Random selection of participants from the general population The main advantage of a randomized controlled trial (RTC) compared to all other epidemiology study designs is that the RCT: equally distributes characteristics that may be independent risk factors for the outcome of interest over the study arms. is prospective thereby eliminating the need for historical data. is less expensive. guarantees that confounding bias will not occur. A pharmaceutical company showed the following in an article: \"1500 subjects with a cold were treated with our new medicine. Within three days, 95% were asymptomatic and this result was statistically significant.\" The company claims the new medicine was effective. Is this conclusion justified? Yes, because the effect was very large (95% of the subjects benefitted from treatment). No, because statistical significance indicates that the null hypothesis (\"no effect\") was correct. No, because no control group was involved in the study. Yes, because the effect of treatment could not be explained by chance. Public health surveillance includes various activities. Which one is not part of public health surveillance? Data collection Data dissemination Disease control Data analysis The problem of confounding can be solved by... choosing a prospective design. increasing the precision of the measurements. stratification during data analysis. this cannot be solved. In the definition of epidemiology, \"distribution\" refers to: Who When Where Why In the definition of epidemiology, \"determinants\" generally includes: Agents Causes Control measures Risk factors Sources John Snow's investigation of cholera is considered a model for epidemiologic field investigations because it included a: Biologically plausible hypothesis Comparison of a health outcome among exposed and unexposed groups Multivariate statistical model Spot map Recommendation for public health action Public health surveillance includes which of the following activities: Diagnosing whether a case of encephalitis is actually due to West Nile virus infection Soliciting case reports of persons with symptoms compatible with SARs from local hospitals Creating graphs of the number of dog bites by week and neighborhood Writing a report on trends in seat belt use to share with the state legislature Disseminating educational materials about ways people can reduce their risk of Lyme disease The hallmark feature of an analytic epidemiologic study is: (Choose one best answer) Use of an appropriate comparison group Laboratory confirmation of the diagnosis Publication in a peer-reviewed journal Statistical analysis using logistic regression Aspecific case definition is one that: Is likely to include only (or mostly) true cases Is considered \"loose\" or \"broad\" Will include more cases than a sensitive case definition May exclude mild cases British investigators conducted a study to compare measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine history among 1,294 children with pervasive development disorder (e.g., autism and Asperger's syndrome) and 4,469 children without such disorders. (They found no association.) This is an example of which type(s) of study? Experimental Observational Cohort Case-control Clinical trial A cohort study differs from a case-control study in that: Subjects are enrolled or categorized on the basis of their exposure status in a cohort study but not in a case-control study Subjects are asked about their exposure status in a cohort study but not in a case-control study Cohort studies require many years to conduct, but case-control studies do not Cohort studies are conducted to investigate chronic diseases, case-control studies are used for infectious diseases A key feature of a cross-sectional study is that: It usually provides information on prevalence rather than incidence It is limited to health exposures and behaviors rather than health outcomes It is more useful for descriptive epidemiology than it is for analytic epidemiology It is synonymous with survey The strength of an association between exposure and disease is best measured by the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"incidence rate of disease in the exposed group"},{"label":"B","text":"attributable risk"},{"label":"C","text":"attributable fraction"},{"label":"D","text":"population attributable risk"},{"label":"E","text":"relative risk"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1aba6d1a1040","stem":"In the first 12 years of follow-up of the Framingham Heart Study, the observed number of cases of angina was 1.6 times higher than the number expected based on population rates. What type of measure is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"prevalence rate"},{"label":"B","text":"incidence rate ratio"},{"label":"C","text":"risk ratio"},{"label":"D","text":"adjusted incidence rate"},{"label":"E","text":"standardized incidence (or morbidity) ratio"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a5aca08aada","stem":"In a study of alcohol and oral cancer the relative risk is 2.0 for men and 2.0 for women but 4.0 for both sexes combined. This suggests that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"there is confounding by sex in these data"},{"label":"B","text":"there is confounding by some unknown or unmeasured factor in these data"},{"label":"C","text":"there is evidence of effect modification in these data"},{"label":"D","text":"the results have been adjusted for age and sex"},{"label":"E","text":"the results are due to bias"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-320855f13bb1","stem":"The following table shows data from an epidemiological study. What type of study was this most likely to be? Number of episodes Person-years (py) at risk Exposure Present 700 1950 Absent 300 2250 Total 1000 4200","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"case-control"},{"label":"B","text":"cohort"},{"label":"C","text":"randomised controlled trial"},{"label":"D","text":"cross-sectional"},{"label":"E","text":"descriptive"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-84b31d274cf2","stem":"Based on question 43 above, what is the incidence rate among those who are exposed to the factor under study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Incidence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"35.9 per 100 py"},{"label":"B","text":"35.9 per 100,000 py"},{"label":"C","text":"35.9 per 1000 py"},{"label":"D","text":"13.3 per 100,000 py"},{"label":"E","text":"23.8 per 100 py"},{"label":"F","text":"none of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-033b8f768844","stem":"Based on question 43 above, what is the rate difference?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.6 per 100 py"},{"label":"B","text":"22.6 per 100,000 py"},{"label":"C","text":"12.1 per 100,000 py"},{"label":"D","text":"10.5 per 100 py"},{"label":"E","text":"2.7 f. 0.37"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40c7052f9715","stem":"Based on 43 above, what is the population attributable risk?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.6 per 100 py"},{"label":"B","text":"22.6 per 100,000 py"},{"label":"C","text":"2.7 d. 12.1 per 100 py"},{"label":"E","text":"10.5 per 100 py"},{"label":"F","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3fb378b2d891","stem":"Which two of the following methods cannot be used to prevent confounding from occurring in a study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"randomization"},{"label":"B","text":"restriction"},{"label":"C","text":"stratification"},{"label":"D","text":"matching"},{"label":"E","text":"multivariable analysis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-60e6e646c746","stem":"A randomized, placebo-controlled trial was conducted in Indonesia to study the effects of Vitamin A in preventing deaths among children with measles. The investigators reported a relative risk of 0.60 for the intervention versus control group. This means that To assess the association between Kawasaki syndrome (KS) and carpet shampoo, investigators conducted a case-control study with 100 cases (children with KS) and 100 controls (children without KS). Among the children with KS, 50 had a history of recent exposure to carpet shampoo. Among the controls, the number with a recent history of exposure to carpet shampoo was 25. For this study, the odds ratio was: What type of case-control study within this cohort would be ideal to study multiple outcomes, and why is the alternative case-control design not recommended? case-cohort design would be ideal, as the same control group, represented by a sample of the total cohort at baseline could be used as comparison group to multiple case groups. If a nested case-control study design were chosen, a different control group would have to be selected for each case group, which would not be cost-effective. In this cohort study, prevalent cases were not excluded at baseline and, thus the investigators chose to use baseline data to examine associations between suspected risk factors and prevalent disease. What type of approach is this, and what are its main advantages and disadvantages? tions of associations that can later be confirmed in longitudinal analyses (that is they allow generation of hypotheses). The disadvantage is that point prevalence ratios may be good estimates of the relative risks, as prevalence is a reflection of both incidence and duration of the disease after diagnosis. Although cross-sectional results may also inform decisions about what measures to add or to drop from follow-up examinations, epidemiologists should be mindful of the prevalence-incidence bias. In planning an individually matched case-based case-control study to test the hypothesis that air pollution (measured by individually placed monitors) is related to a certain type of respiratory cancer. The investigators decide to match cases and controls on age, gender, ethnic background, and smoking (yes or no). In additional to general logical difficulties usually associated with matching. What is the main undesirable consequence that may result from matching cases and controls in this study? y matching on ethnic background, the investigators will also match on variables related to ethnic background, which may include residence area. As a result, cases and controls may be “overmatched” that is, they may be matched on the exposure of interest. Because the disease of interest is rare, the investigators decide to individually match 1o controls for each case. Is this reasonable strategy, considering the additional costs involved and the tight budget to conduct this study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Odds Ratio","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"children receiving vitamin a were 40% less likely to die from measles than children receiving placebo"},{"label":"B","text":"the chance of dying from measles in the placebo group was 60%"},{"label":"C","text":"the chance of the null hypothesis being true is 60%"},{"label":"D","text":"60% of the children who died received vitamin a; the other 40% received placebo"},{"label":"E","text":"none of the above statements are true"},{"label":"A","text":"1.0"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0"},{"label":"D","text":"cannot be calculated from the information given"},{"label":"B","text":"Basic design in analytical epidemiology: A case-control is conducted within a well-defined cohort. The reason for this is that expensive additional data collection is needed, and the budget is not sufficient to obtain this data from all cohort participants."},{"label":"B","text":"Measures of Disease Occurrence and association"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ef4451e50ff","stem":"6 Total 318 100 393 100 Using absolute number of cases and controls with and without a history of hypertension. Calculate the absolute odds of history of hypertension separately in cases and controls. Odds of hypertension history: cases=70/248, = 0.28 Odds of hypertension history: controls=30/363, = 0.08 Now calculate the odds of hypertension using the percentages of cases and controls with a history of hypertension. Odds of hypertension history: cases=22/78, = 0.28 Odds of hypertension history: controls=7.6/92.4, = 0.08 What can you conclude from comparing the response to a) to the response 2b)? Why are the odds of a history of hypertension more similar to the proportion of individuals with history of hypertension in controls than in cases? ecause the proportion (prevalence) of history of a hypertension is much lower in controls (7.6%) than in cases (22%). The odds tend to approximate the proportion when the proportion is low. Epidemiological Study Designs","type":"Calculation","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc9f817f380c","stem":"What are the applications and disadvantages of the major epidemiological study designs?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06d25f339fa6","stem":"Outline the design of a case-control study and a cohort study to examine the association of a high-fat diet with bowel cancer.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Nutrition and Dietetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Nutrition and Dietetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3db332e33eb2","stem":"What is random error and how can it be reduced?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6c89c4aea1c3","stem":"What are the main types of systematic error in epidemiological studies and how can their effects be reduced?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0aee231a2d8e","stem":"Describe in which studies the relative risk (RR) and the odds ratio (OR) are used. Outline the reasons why they would be used in a particular study and not in another.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Odds Ratio","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-311f3e77e360","stem":"A cross-sectional study of Downs syndrome has found an association with birth order. What could be a cause of confounding and how would you avoid it?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d6bcedd2e9bf","stem":"At the start of a cohort study the exposure is determined with the help of a questionnaire. During the study there is no \"loss to follow-up\". At the end of the follow-up time the number of cases is known and is divided into exposed and unexposed. The odds ratio (OR) is used as the association measure. Which comment is the most appropriate here? Anwer: a","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Odds Ratio","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-427828e13e8c","stem":"What is de incidence rate in this study? Answer: b","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Incidence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_