ltiple regression?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"One"},{"label":"B","text":"One or more"},{"label":"C","text":"Two or more"},{"label":"D","text":"Two"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"932734ed9ebca39f6a33776ff44bf2d714fd786a","manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-594978249b6f","stem":"Which of the following represents the fiftieth percentile, or the middle point in a set of numbers arranged in order of magnitude?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mode"},{"label":"B","text":"Median"},{"label":"C","text":"Mean"},{"label":"D","text":"Variance"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ee406b5d8614","stem":"In a grouped frequency distribution, the intervals should be what?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutually exclusive"},{"label":"B","text":"Exhaustive"},{"label":"C","text":"Both A and B"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither A nor B"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5aa2d5ca5488","stem":"When a set of numbers is heterogeneous, you can place more trust in the measure of central tendency as representing the typical person or unit.","type":"True / False","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e861add900d8","stem":"Which of the following is suitable specimen for blood grouping of foetus at birth? Cord blood. Venous blood. Capillary blood. Serum from venous blood. Serumfrom capillary blood. 4 Which of the following means checkinghow close several results from the same specimen are to each other? Precision check. Carry over check. Calibration check. Standard deviation check. Coefficient of variation check. 5Minimum recommended haemoglobin concentration for female donor is","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a387135b6d08","stem":"The type of antibodies that cause clumping of red blood cells when the blood temperature falls below normal body temperature is called Rouleaux antibodies Compatibility antibodies Cold agglutinin Agglutination antibodies Autoimmune antibodies The liquid portion of human blood that has been frozen and preserved 24 hours fter blood donation is called Frozen plasma Fresh frozen plasma Cryoprecipitate Packed red blood cells Plasma During compatibility testing, presence of agglutination(clumping) indicates: The donor and recipient are compatible The donor and recipient are incompatible The blood has clotted The donor and recipients blood groups are the same None of the above Woman of blood group A who has been married by a man with the same blood group (O) are suspecting to produce an offspring of which blood group?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b085f5aa448","stem":"Family unpaid Blood Donors are also known as:- Un paid Blood Donors; Autologous Blood Donors; Professional Blood Donors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"D","text":"Replacement Blood Donors"},{"label":"E","text":"Free donors The possible off- spring (children) ABO phenotype blood groups, from group A and group B Parents are: Group – A, group – B, group –AB and group – O Group- A, group – B and group –A B Group – A, group – B and group- O Group – AB and group – O Group – AB Which one among the followings immunoglobulin is the most cause of haemolytic disease of new born. IgA IgD IgE IgG IgM Replacement blood donors are also known as follows Paid blood donors Voluntary non remunerated blood donors Family unpaid donors Unpaid blood donors Paid commercial"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ee1a7732b973","stem":"Best blood product to be used for patients with severe bleeding due to dysfunction of clotting mechanism is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole blood."},{"label":"B","text":"Packed red cells."},{"label":"C","text":"Packed white cells."},{"label":"D","text":"Fresh frozen plasma."},{"label":"E","text":"Frozen plasma blood."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e6c7553e7a9d","stem":"A person of blood group A, possess the following on the surface of red blood cell Antigen B Antibodies A and B Antigens A and B Antigen A NONE Woman of blood group O who has been married by a man with the same blood group (O) are suspecting to produce an offspring of which blood group?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood group A and B"},{"label":"B","text":"Only blood group O"},{"label":"C","text":"Blood group O and AB"},{"label":"D","text":"Blood group A,B,AB and O"},{"label":"E","text":"Only blood group B The term hematology can be simply defined as: A study of how blood can be transfused A number of blood cells found in human being A study of blood and its constituents A study of WBCs and their function A study of how one inherit his/her blood group One of the following blood group is termed as UNIVERSAL DONOR AB O A B NONE OF THE ABOVE A substance on the surface of red blood cell that stimulate the production of antibodies is called Antibody Platelet Immunoglobulin Antigen Serum A person of blood group A can receive blood from which blood groups: A and O B and O AB and O ONLY AB"},{"label":"E","text":"All blood groups Genes which are expressed phenotypically and can be passed from parents to offspring’s are termed as:- Recessive genes. Dominant genes. Homozygous genes. Co-dominant genes. Heterozygous genes. Unpaid blood donors are also known as : Autologous blood donors Replacement blood donors Family unpaid blood donors Voluntary remunerated blood donors Voluntary non remunerated blood donors One among the following statement explains the aim of establishing criteria of suitable blood donors. To reduce fear among blood givers To protect blood givers in the field To protect blood givers and blood receivers To make blood givers know the phlebotomist To make blood givers know blood transfusion services Providing the knowledge about the consequences of the laboratory test results fall under one of the following criteria of selecting suitable blood donors: Medical history Health check criteria Post donation counseling Pre-donation counseling Blood donor survey criteria Among the following which one is Rhesus antigen but cannot stimulate formation of specific antibody. d antigen D antigen C antigen e antigen E antigen Blood cells capable of ameboid movement in a human body are Leucocytes Erythrocytes Thrombocytes Plasma cells Casts cells Raised reticulocyte count in an individual can predict of one of the following kind of anaemia: Folate deficiency anaemia associated with Megaloblastic changes Haemolyticanaemia associated with non Megaloblastic changes Vitamin B 12 deficiency anaemia associated with Megaloblastic changes Vitamin B 12 deficiency anaemia associated with non Megaloblastic changes Liver disease associated with Megaloblastic changes At the end of the RBCs life span they are removed by. Hemolysis Phagocyte of the reticuloendothelium system Degenaration Bone marrow depression Erythropoiesis . Leucocytes are nucleated cells which are much less numerous than Platelets Erythrocytes Monocytes Basophils Target cells Hypoxic death can be contributed by Oxygen saturation High haemoglobin concentration Erythropoesis Leucopenia Carbon monoxide poisoning When abnormal beta gene is inherited from both parent(homozygous state), the disorder is mainly referred as Anaemia Incomplete Sickle call Sickle cell anemia Sickle cell trait Inheritance Haemoglobin that results from the replacement of Glutamic acid with valine in 6th position of beta haemoglobin chainis known as: Haemoglobin C Haemoglobin O Arab Haemoglobin S Ferrous form of haemoglobin Haemoglobin D punjab Anaemia that results from the Vernomous snake bite is classified under the following types of anaemia: Inherited hemolytic anaemia Immune acquired Hemolytic anaemia Hemoglobinopathies related anaemia Non immune acquired hemolytic anaemia Inherited non immune acquired hemolytic anemia One of the following hemoglobin pigment can occur as a result of an inherited abnormality of the haemoglobin molecule: Carboxyhaemoglobin Methaemoglobin Thalassemia Oxyhaemoglobin Sulphaemoglobin When abnormal beta gene is inherited from one parent and normal beta gene from another parent(heterozygous state), the disorder is mainly referred as Anaemia Sickle cell disease Sickle cell anemia Sickle cell trait Inheritance One of the following cells is characterized by a BICONCAVE disc shape: Neutrophils Monocytes Erythrocytes Lymphocytes Thrombocytes A solution used to prevent rupture of cells made by sodium chloride is sometimes termed as: Isotonic solution Hypotonic solution Distilled water Sodium hydroxide solution Disinfectant The average diameter of red blood cell is approximately:"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-22947711b49b","stem":"For quality Blood and Blood product storage, which one is property of Blood transfusion refrigerator?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"55d61703af85de34ab4ddb697d94453bda2ee970"},{"id":"q-243d8ddcfacc","stem":"List four types of Investigations (tests) which should be done, before a unit of Blood from the Blood Bank Refrigerator, is transfused to a Patient. Screening for Transfusion Transmissible Infections (TTIs) ABO Grouping by Tile or Tube method Rh Grouping by Tile or Tube method. Compatibility testing / cross matching by tube method (Immediate spin or 30 to 60 minutes incubation at Room temperature, 30◦c & 37◦c including IAT).","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2d93ce85244","stem":"List common transfusion transmissible infectious AIDS Syphilis Hepatitis Malaria Trypanosomiasis","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fbae0a636c7","stem":"Enumerate five (5) blood products which can be transfused to patients. Packed red cells Fresh frozen plasma Platelets Cryoprecipitate","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe105d58a716","stem":"Mention FIVE materials used to control cold chain in blood donation Cold box, Blood transfusion refrigerator, Thermometer, Temperature control chat, Alarm system, Ice packs Auto-alternative source of power.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd44281ef56a","stem":"Mention four(4) common ABO blood groups A blood group B blood group AB blood group O blood group","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54990fc4ac29","stem":"Explain on five (5) diseases that should be investigated in blood unit before it is transfused to another person. AIDS Syphilis Hepatitis Malaria Trypanosomiasis","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-23e5970679cf","stem":"Explain causes of false positive results in ABO Blood grouping CAUSES OF FALSE POSITIVE RESULTS IN ABO BLOOD GROUPING ROULEAUX FORMATION Rouleaux formation is sometimes called ‘pseudo-agglutination. Rouleaux formation is not true agglutination, because it is not caused by an absorbable agglutinin. It does not usually give trouble to an experienced worker. When rouleaux formation is marked, the auto-agglutination control tube will be positive and any cells added to the patient’s serum will give a similar reaction. However if the serum is diluted 1 in 2 or 1 in 3 with physiological saline, the rouleaux formation should disappear, but true agglutination will persist. Rouleaux formation may cause a mistake when reading results in tile method if reading is delayed. A ‘graininess’ appears which may be read as a weak positive. The graininess will disappear when a drop of saline or albumin is added to the mixture. INFECTED RED CELLS (THE THOMSEN PHENOMENON) Several types of bacteria are capable of causing red cells to agglutinate by any normal serum, except young infants’ serum. This phenomenon is called polyagglutination and the cells are called polyagglutinable. The bacteria expose the T antigen in the cells and most sera contain the T antibody and therefore agglutination occurs. The reaction may appear within 18 h at 4oc or within shorter periods at room temperature. It is important to use fresh cells or cells which have been stored for the shortest time in the refrigerator when doing blood grouping. In blood stored in the form of a clot, the phenomenon is rarely found. INFECTED SERUM Occasionally this gives a false positive reaction, but in most cases it gives a false negative reaction. COLD AGGLUTININS Cold agglutinins will usually react at temperatures not higher than 250c. If they are active at room temperature the auto-agglutination control will be positive as well as the A, B and O cells. This type of reaction is termed pan-agglutination. High titre cold antibodies are sometimes formed during viral or atypical pneumonia e.g. infection by Mycoplasma pneumonia, infectious mononucleosis or in lymphomas. When this type of reaction is encountered, the patient’s red cells should be washed in warm saline at 37oc to remove the cold antibody, and the test repeated. The serum grouping must be repeated at 37oc and the auto-agglutination control should be negative, although at this temperature any anti-A or Anti-B present will still react.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ccdff87bb95b","stem":"Explain risk behaviours for Transfusion Transmissible Infections A Having more than one sexual partner B Prostitution/commercial sex work C Homosexual (man having sex with other Man) D Bisexual (having both sex with man and woman) E Injecting drug use F Skin scarification and tattooing You have been posted to work with Zonal blood bank in Zone Y as a Laboratory quality officer. One day your Zonal manager assigned you to transport blood to the Regional Hospitals within your Zone. Describe how will you maintain the cold chain process for transported blood according to standard operating procedure Introduction A cold chain is the systematic process for the safe storage and transportation of blood from its collection from the donor to it administration to a patient who requires transfusion. Main body The following are necessary tools for maintaining cold chain of transported blood. Cool box: Should be insulated and built and designated to take ice in the centre and temperature maintained at 4-6oC for at least six (6) hours Special thermometer: Is wrapped and placed in the cool box to monitor minimum and maximum temperature during transportation. The thermometer should not be in contact with ice packs. Conclusion: If the finest and most modern equipment and vehicles are available, the blood chain will not be effective if people do not handle properly the tools. Health check as criteria during blood donation processes involves checking for various parameters to ensure the safety of donor him/herself and blood donated, Explain on parameters to be assessed so that the donor can qualify for blood donation. ANSWER Blooddonation is a process by which a blood donor voluntarily has blood drawn for storage in a blood bank for subsequent use in a blood transfusion Age Weight Hemoglobin concentration Last donation date History of chronic disease Time of the last meal Blood pressure Physical assessment of the donor for tattoo and scars CONCLUSION Record management as a part of quality assurance should be highly observed during blood donation and transfusion services; this involves proper and correct labeling and recording of donated units as well as recording those positive and negative units for TTI. States the two laws of heredity Offspring cannot possess antigen A or B antigens alone or in combination except that it is inherited from one or both parents. The parent of group AB cannot produce an offspring of groups O nor can a parent of group O give rise to a child of groups AB. This is because the AB is a heterozygous so that A gene must come from one parent and the B gene from the other. A rare form called cis AB exists.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13d2581d02fd","stem":"Explain the characteristics of rhesus antibodies Answers Immune antibodies - Rhesus antibodies are always produced due to immunization by previous transfusion or pregnancy. However there are some naturally occurring forms of anti E IgG – Rhesus antibodies are IgG immunoglobulin Optimum temperature – They react favourably at 370C Rhesus antibodies don’t fix complement and do not therefore cause in vitro haemolysis List causes of transfusion reactions. Answers Allergic Anaphylactic Incompatibility / haemolysis Circulatory overload All of these adverse reactions can be harmful to the patient. Incompatibility due to ABO is usually the most severe and can threaten the life of the patient immediately and within minutes of starting the transfusion. Allergic reaction may be most common and often mild. It results in a slight fever or hives. Anaphylactic is less common and can be due to something in the donated blood such as a medication that the patient has a strong allergy with. Circulatory overload happens in older individuals with failing heart when large amounts of packed cells are given. It was more common when whole blood was transfused but can still be a problem. Principle of ABO and Rhesus grouping Answers The principle of test is based upon agglutination test and specific antigen – antibody reaction. The blood group antigens are located on the surface of the red cells and any antibody produced is found in the serum. Known antibody (antisera) are used to detect unknown antigen (cells) If the reaction occurs when the specific antisera is added to a patient (donor red cells) it indicate that antigen present in the red cells surface. Therefore the blood group of the patient or donor can be determined. Centrifugation will enhance the reaction in vitro by enhancing the combining of antigen and antibody in a short period of time.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-18992bef4639","stem":"Which of the following maternal antibodies are associated with Hemolytic disease of the newborn","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ab1a2cfa4bc","stem":"For quality Blood and Blood product storage, which one is property of Blood transfusion refrigerator?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVe