erning myocardial infarction are true except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"may be unrecognizable at autopsy if the patient dies within four or five hours after the infarction"},{"label":"B","text":"need not be associated with vascular occlusion"},{"label":"C","text":"may be followed by systemic arterial occlusions"},{"label":"D","text":"is uncommon in hypertension because of capillary proliferation developed secondary to myocardial hypertrophy"},{"label":"E","text":"is most common in the myocardium supplied by the anterior descending branch of the left coronary artery"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5efc1d712240","stem":"Myocardial infarction may be followed by all of the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"elevated temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"elevated venous pressure"},{"label":"C","text":"elevated sedimentation rate"},{"label":"D","text":"neutropenia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3e2d5832056a","stem":"The most common cause of myocardial infarction is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"luminal narrowing by atherosclerotic material in coronary arteries"},{"label":"B","text":"ostial stenosis of coronary arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"thrombosis on an atheromatous plaque in a coronary artery"},{"label":"D","text":"embolization from a mural thrombus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c937fedbe266","stem":"Life-threatening complications of acute myocardial infarcts include all of the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"extension of the infarct"},{"label":"B","text":"cardiac rhythm disturbance"},{"label":"C","text":"rupture of the myocardium"},{"label":"D","text":"endocardial fibroelastosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58594cd3e434","stem":"A middle-aged male had several episodes of chest pain during the last two years. He was admitted to the hospital following a very severe attack which ended in shock. The pain began about an hour before he was admitted. The most likely outcome in this case is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"survival with reduced cardiac reserve"},{"label":"B","text":"death due to congestive heart failure in the third week"},{"label":"C","text":"rupture of the myocardium"},{"label":"D","text":"cerebral embolism from a mural thrombosis"},{"label":"E","text":"pulmonary embolism"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9bde0db2dfb0","stem":"Each of the following is a complication of a left ventricular aneurysm except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cerebral abscess"},{"label":"B","text":"congestive heart failure"},{"label":"C","text":"left ventricular calcification"},{"label":"D","text":"infarction of bowel"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3967462150f3","stem":"Which of the following most typically occurs at 6 days after onset of a myocardial infarction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"opti