lse,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9f1ec1fd5ab9","stem":"Malarial anemia could be from any, but one, of the following causes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypersplenism especially in tropical splenomegaly syndrome"},{"label":"B","text":"Bone marrow suppression"},{"label":"C","text":"Extravascular destruction of the parasitized red cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Blackwater fever from intravascular destruction of parasitized red cell E Dysplasia of the blood cell precursors in the bone marrow 66 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-49ba818d8141","stem":"All, but one, of the following cancers can produce erythropoietin and cause secondary polycythemia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypernephroma"},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatoma C Leukemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchial carcinoma"},{"label":"E","text":"Pheochromocytoma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a23d4256a3bf","stem":"Basophilic stippling is a characteristic feature of one of the following red cell enzymopathies","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose 6 Phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvate Kinase deficiency"},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphofructokinase deficiency"},{"label":"D","text":"Triose Phosphate isomerase deficiency E Pyrimidine 5 nucleotidase deficiency"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ed2e36b96544","stem":"Type II CDA “Congenital Dyserythropoietic Anemia” has all, but one, of the following features: A It is the least common of the three types of CDA","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"It is transmitted as an autosomal recessive disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Red cells could be hemolysed by acidified serum, hence called HEMPAS"},{"label":"D","text":"Smear shows aniso-poikilocytosis, and marrow erythroid multinuclearity"},{"label":"E","text":"Patients have anemia, mild jaundice and evidence of iron overload"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76cb375fcb97","stem":"A child from Tihama, villages of the southern Saudi Arabia, admitted with a high fever and organomegaly, could need all of the following investigations EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone marrow examination to rule out Burkitt lymphoma B PET scan of the abdomen for hot areas of non-Hodgkin lymphoma lesions"},{"label":"C","text":"Splenic puncture to rule out visceral Leishmaniasis"},{"label":"D","text":"Immunophenotyping of blast cells “if present” for CD41 and CD61"},{"label":"E","text":"Enzyme studies to rule out storage diseases"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-99bf7605fd30","stem":"Osmotic fragility test is helpful in identifying one of the following anemias","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"label":"B","text":"Sickle cell disease C Hereditary spherocytosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Aplastic anemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Diamond Blackfan syndrome Q494 Patients with CHAD “Cold Hemagglutinin Disease” might have all, but one, of the following features:."},{"label":"A","text":"Acrocyanosis in cold weather due to red cell agglutination"},{"label":"B","text":"It is usually a benign chronic elderly disease, though could be distressing"},{"label":"C","text":"The hematology analyzer could record a very high red cell MCV D It is never associated with lymphoma"},{"label":"E","text":"It could follow infection with Mycoplasma pneumoniae 67 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-91d7ae69971e","stem":"All, but one, of the following features should raise the possibility of PNH “Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravascular hemolysis with negative direct Coomb’s test"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolysis with low platelet and/or white blood cell count"},{"label":"C","text":"Hemoglobinuria, Budd-Chiari syndrome or recurrent abdominal pain"},{"label":"D","text":"Pancytopenia with marrow failure E Splenomegaly and hypersplenism"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ffe536d237a4","stem":"Match the following hematological data & clinical features with the corresponding blood disease: Biodata: Age: 44 years Sex: Male Presentation: Two years history of anemia not responding to treatment CBC: Hb: 8.0 gm/dL WBC: 8,000/uL Platelet: 200,000/uL Differential: Normal Blood Smear: Mildly macrocytic anemia Marrow aspiration: Active trilineage hemopoiesis Others: No evidence of megaloblastic anemia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Fanconi’s anemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory anemia with excessive blasts"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia E Refractory anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d5894c47d6f","stem":"Match the following hematological data & clinical features with the corresponding blood disease: Biodata: Age: 80 years Sex: Male Presentation: Left hypochondrial fullness CBC: Hb: 10.0 gm/dL WBC: 80,000/uL Platelet: 200,000/uL Differential: Neutrophils:60% Monocytes:10% Myelocytes:10% Blasts:4% Blood Smear: Neutrophilia and monocytosis Marrow aspiration: Hypercellular with some dysplastic features On Examination: Marked splenomegaly","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Fanconi’s anemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory anemia with excessive blasts D Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Refractory anemia 68 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-02a990740f76","stem":"Match the following hematological data & clinical features with the corresponding blood disease: Biodata: Age: 8 years Sex: Male Presentation: Anemia, infections & bruising CBC: Hb: 8.0 gm/dL WBC: 1,000/uL Platelet: 25,000/uL Differential: Neutrophils = 80% Blood Smear: Oligopenic Marrow aspiration: Nearly empty Others: Horse-shoe shaped kidney; extra thumb; café-au-lait spots.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia B Fanconi’s anemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory anemia with excessive blasts"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Refractory anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a24754c403d4","stem":"Match the following hematological data & clinical features with the corresponding blood diseases: Biodata: Age: 76 years Sex: Male Presentation: Anemia, infection and bleeding CBC: Hb: 6.0 gm/dL WBC: 2,000/uL Platelet: 60,000/uL Differential: Blasts = 12% Neutrophils = 44% Blood Smear: Hypogranular neutrophils; macrocytosis Marrow aspiration: Trilineage dysplasia; marrow blasts = 18% Others: Patient died six months later from an intracranial bleed. Blasts were still 12%","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Fanconi’s anemia C Refractory anemia with excessive blasts"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Refractory anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-83c63f5afbd1","stem":"Match the following hematological data & clinical features with the corresponding blood diseases: Biodata: Age: 6 years Sex: Male Presentation: Anemia, infection and bleeding CBC: Hb: 6.0 gm/dL WBC: 60,000 /uL Platelet: 60,000 /uL Differential: Blasts = 44% Neutrophils = 8 Others= 48% Blood Smear: Blast cells with high nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio Marrow aspiration: Solidly cellular with blast cells constituting majority of the cells Others: Blast cells were positive for CD10; TdT & HLA-DR A Pro-B acute lymphoblastic leukemia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Burkitt leukemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory anemia with excessive blasts"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"label":"E","text":"T- acute lymphoblastic leukemia 69 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ ONE THOUSAND HEMATOLOGY MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS EXAMINATION BANK Professor Anwar Sheikha MD, FRCP, FRCPI, FRCPA, FCAP, FRCPath., FACP “501 to 1000” Select the most appropriate answer:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d17170cadd54","stem":"Presence of “Elliptocytes” is not a usual finding in one of the following blood diseases","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Megaloblastic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Myelofibrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary elliptocytosis D Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"label":"E","text":"Severe iron deficiency anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-144777b42d46","stem":"Spherocytosis is not a usual feature in one of the following hemolytic anemias","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warm auto-immune hemolytic anemias"},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary spherocytosis C Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolytic disease of the newborn"},{"label":"E","text":"Clostridial septicemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4e837bfbba8e","stem":"Spherocytes could hardly be seen in one of the following conditions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"After transfusion of stored blood"},{"label":"C","text":"Severe sepsis with disseminated intravascular hemolysis D Aplastic anemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Blood film prepared from blood allowed to stand before making the smear"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e63b49efeb4e","stem":"Anisochromasia (various populations of red blood cells with different hemoglobin content) is not a feature of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sideroblastic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Transfused iron deficient anemia patients"},{"label":"C","text":"Treated iron deficient anemia patients"},{"label":"D","text":"A previously normal patient after one month of bleeding E Acute post-hemorrhagic anemia 70 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6bd43c1ed507","stem":"In severe polycythemia rubra vera: A Marrow iron stain is almost always absent","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Philadelphia chromosome is almost always positive"},{"label":"C","text":"Hemoglobin is almost always high even after repeated venesections"},{"label":"D","text":"Splenomegaly is almost always present"},{"label":"E","text":"ESR “Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate” is almost always high"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-236a946c7110","stem":"In severe polycythemia rubra vera","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet count is usually above a million per uL"},{"label":"B","text":"White cell count is always in hundreds of thousands per uL C Red cell count is always high"},{"label":"D","text":"Patients always have bleeding and thrombosis at the same time"},{"label":"E","text":"All patients are symptomatic at presentation"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91637e764354","stem":"Megakaryocyte hyperplasia with abnormal morphology is not a feature of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Essential thrombocythemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (M7)"},{"label":"C","text":"Myelofibrosis"},{"label":"D","text":"5q- syndrome E Iron deficiency anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b2d9008ddf6","stem":"Marrow megakaryocyte hyperplasia is not a feature of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypersplenism causing thrombocytopenia"},{"label":"C","text":"Polycythemia rubra vera"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myeloid leukemia E Drug-induced non-immune thrombocytopenia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-794cc7770c1d","stem":"When treating patients with iron deficiency anemias","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron should be given as a second-line measure because blood transfusion is more effective and a quicker mode of therapy"},{"label":"B","text":"Iron should be given until the hemoglobin is corrected"},{"label":"C","text":"Iron should not be given beyond the correction of the anemia to avoid iron overload D Iron should be continued beyond anemia correction to replenish the stores"},{"label":"E","text":"Parenteral iron is a quicker, better and safer mode of therapy"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-56aab9bdf9ad","stem":"One of the following features is not true in thalassemia minor patients","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are not infrequently diagnosed as iron deficiency and given iron B High RDW is helpful in differentiating it from iron deficiency anemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Patients are usually asymptomatic from their disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Offspring could develop thalassemia major if they get married to a similar patient"},{"label":"E","text":"High HbA2 is the best test to diagnose β- thalassemia minor"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ac5895a778d1","stem":"One of the followings is true about Red Cell Distribution Width (RDW)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"RDW is normal in iron deficiency anemia B RDW is usually normal in thalassemia minor"},{"label":"C","text":"Low MCV & high RDW favor the diagnosis of thalassemia minor"},{"label":"D","text":"The normal RDW should not drop below 14.5% in the Coulter hemogram"},{"label":"E","text":"RDW does not have any role in differentiating iron deficiency from thalassemia 71 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-40f64369db15","stem":"One of the followings is not correct in relation to spleen size in sickle cell disease: A Spleen is atrophic at birth","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"By 6 months of age 1/3rd of patients have splenomegaly"},{"label":"C","text":"By 12 months of age 2/3rd of patients have splenomegaly"},{"label":"D","text":"By 2 years of age 3/4th of patients have splenomegaly"},{"label":"E","text":"Most patients will develop splenic atrophy when they get older"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-651325f6ce81","stem":"All but one of the following primary measures are effective in sickle cell anemia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxyurea B Blood transfusion for all anemic patients"},{"label":"C","text":"Partial exchange transfusion for special circumstances like major surgery"},{"label":"D","text":"Analgesia and morphia for painful vaso-occlusive crises"},{"label":"E","text":"Strict control of infection"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a708de33e800","stem":"All but one of the following features relate to thalassemia minor patients","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They could have a normal hemoglobin concentration!"},{"label":"B","text":"Differs from iron deficiency anemia by having a more normal RDW"},{"label":"C","text":"Usually asymptomatic clinically"},{"label":"D","text":"They should be advised to avoid marriage with similar carriers E High altitude could trigger pain, hemolysis and anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c7f4cb0d46a","stem":"One of the following features is not true in relations to Heinz bodies","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are red cell membrane-bound denatured hemoglobin B They are nuclear remnants"},{"label":"C","text":"They could be seen in unstable hemoglobins"},{"label":"D","text":"Blister cells are thought to be from splenic removal of Heinz bodies"},{"label":"E","text":"They are best detected using Supravital rather than Romanowsky stains"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2a11df7f1cd0","stem":"One of the following features is true in relation to Heinz bodies: A It could be detected in Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"It could be detected in Evans’ syndrome"},{"label":"C","text":"It is one of the features of megaloblastic anemias"},{"label":"D","text":"It could be seen in sideroblastic anemias"},{"label":"E","text":"It is a feature of Pyrimidine-5-Nucleotidase deficiency"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3c9c7996d736","stem":"Intravascular hemolysis is a well-known complication of any of the followings EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABO incompatible blood transfusion"},{"label":"B","text":"PNH “Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria”"},{"label":"C","text":"CHAD “Cold Hemagglutinin Disease”"},{"label":"D","text":"G-6-P DH “Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase” deficiency E Systemic lupus erythematosus with autoimmune hemolytic anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b93abd63930","stem":"Patients are plethoric with ruddy complexion and suffused conjunctiva in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Essential thrombocythemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Myelofibrosis C Polycythemia rubra vera"},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplastic syndromes"},{"label":"E","text":"Chronic myeloid leukemia 72 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fbd5426e028d","stem":"All of followings relate to LAP “Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase” score” EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Could range between “0” & “400”"},{"label":"B","text":"Normal score should be between “10” and a “100” C It is characteristically high in CML “Chronic Myeloid Leukemia”"},{"label":"D","text":"It is usually high in leukemoid reactions"},{"label":"E","text":"It is high in myelofibrosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d5c1f940b25","stem":"LAP “Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase” score” is best performed on: A Fresh finger-stick blood smears","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"EDTA blood"},{"label":"C","text":"Heparinized blood"},{"label":"D","text":"Citrate blood"},{"label":"E","text":"Clotted blood"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39c0a263a2aa","stem":"All but one of the followings are true about Anemia of Chronic Disorders “ACD”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The anemia is usually normocytic normochromic"},{"label":"B","text":"The anemia could be hypochromic C Megaloblastic marrow changes are common"},{"label":"D","text":"Erythropoietin is usually inadequate"},{"label":"E","text":"ESR is usually raised"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da7f2327fbd3","stem":"Anemia of chronic disorders could be seen in all but one of the following conditions: A Chronic atrial fibrillation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Tuberculosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Malignancy"},{"label":"D","text":"Uremia"},{"label":"E","text":"Cirrhosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-09ff1752d1ee","stem":"All of the followings are true about iron in Anemia of Chronic Disorders EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum iron is usually low B Serum ferritin is usually low"},{"label":"C","text":"TIBC “Total Iron Binding Capacity” is usually low"},{"label":"D","text":"Marrow iron stores are increased or normal"},{"label":"E","text":"No siderotic granules are seen in the developing marrow erythroblasts"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ae86812c3612","stem":"The cause of anemia & particularly hypochromia in anemia of chronic disorders is: A Reticulo-endothelial block from high hepcidin level preventing iron delivery from stores to the erythron","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Iron deficiency"},{"label":"C","text":"Ring sideroblasts with mitochondrial iron imprisonment"},{"label":"D","text":"Poor globin chain synthesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyridoxine deficiency"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1ca1429f41ac","stem":"All of the followings are management options in aplastic anemia EXCEPT: A Hematinics (iron, B12, Folate, etc)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Blood transfusions"},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow transplantation"},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-lymphocyte globulin"},{"label":"E","text":"Cyclosporine & Anti-lymphocyte globulin 73 Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hemato